{"name":"Mélange Green","hex":"#c4c476","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mélange","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of melange."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/m%C3%A9lange"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mélange","blocks":[{"text":"In geology, a mélange is a large-scale breccia, a mappable body of rock characterized by a lack of continuous bedding and the inclusion of fragments of rock of all sizes, contained in a fine-grained deformed matrix. The mélange typically consists of a jumble of large blocks of varied lithologies. Both tectonic and sedimentary processes can form mélange."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Narooma_Breccia.JPG/330px-Narooma_Breccia.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/M%C3%A9lange","wikidata":"Q1795187"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Shale_matrix_m%C3%A9lange_with_clasts_of_sandstone_and_greenstone_on_Marshall%27s_Beach%2C_San_Francisco%2C_USA.jpg/500px-Shale_matrix_m%C3%A9lange_with_clasts_of_sandstone_and_greenstone_on_Marshall%27s_Beach%2C_San_Francisco%2C_USA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Shale_matrix_m%C3%A9lange_with_clasts_of_sandstone_and_greenstone_on_Marshall%27s_Beach%2C_San_Francisco%2C_USA.jpg/1280px-Shale_matrix_m%C3%A9lange_with_clasts_of_sandstone_and_greenstone_on_Marshall%27s_Beach%2C_San_Francisco%2C_USA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Shale_matrix_m%C3%A9lange_with_clasts_of_sandstone_and_greenstone_on_Marshall%27s_Beach%2C_San_Francisco%2C_USA.jpg/500px-Shale_matrix_m%C3%A9lange_with_clasts_of_sandstone_and_greenstone_on_Marshall%27s_Beach%2C_San_Francisco%2C_USA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Shale matrix mélange with clasts of sandstone and greenstone on Marshall's Beach, San Francisco, US","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AShale_matrix_m%C3%A9lange_with_clasts_of_sandstone_and_greenstone_on_Marshall's_Beach%2C_San_Francisco%2C_USA.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9c/Ser